## Context

`tests/test_main.sh` had 5 cases using the double-run anti-pattern: capture
output with `|| true`, then re-run to check exit code. `tests/test_check.sh`
had 1 case (lines 160-166) that ran `erg check` twice: once to capture output
for a WARNING grep, then again to check exit 0.

Double-run is wasteful (extra subprocess) and fragile (second run may behave
differently, e.g. due to side effects).

## Actions

- `test_main.sh`: replace all 5 double-runs with `if out=$(...); then` pattern,
  which captures both output and exit code in a single invocation.
- `test_check.sh`: replace double-run at lines 160-170 with
  `rc=0; out=$(...) || rc=$?` to capture output and exit code once, then make
  two separate pass/fail decisions.

## Exit criteria

- No `$ERG` command appears twice within the same test case block
- `make test` passes (all suites)
